3. Backend Requirements
Server Setup

Node.js + Express.js API server

.env for configs (Kafka brokers, port, etc.)

CORS enabled for React frontend

Kafka Integration

Use kafkajs for Kafka producer & consumer

Topics:

socket-messages → for actual WebSocket data

socket-metrics → for throughput, latency, error stats

Implement Kafka event bus so WebSocket events are published to Kafka

WebSocket Layer

Use Socket.IO (or ws) server

Maintain in-memory map of connected clients with metadata (IP, ID, join time)

On each message:

Broadcast to relevant clients

Publish message event to Kafka

Emit metrics periodically to Kafka

API Routes

/metrics → Returns latest metrics

/connections → Returns list of current clients

/health → Basic health check

Metrics Logging

Store metrics in-memory (or Redis later)

Prometheus-compatible endpoint /metrics

4. Frontend Requirements
React Setup

Use Vite for fast dev server

No TypeScript — only .jsx

TailwindCSS for styling

State Management

Redux Toolkit or Zustand

Store: connections, messages, metrics

WebSocket Client

Connect to backend Socket.IO server

Listen for connection_update, message_update, and metrics_update events

Visualization

Use D3.js (or vis.js) to render:

Force-directed graph for connections

Dynamic edge animations for messages

Color-code edges by latency or error rate

Show “top talkers” panel

Pages

/ → Main Dashboard (graph + stats)

/metrics → Detailed charts (Grafana embed possible)

5. DevOps Setup
Docker: Multi-container (docker-compose.yml with backend, frontend, Kafka, Zookeeper)

Kubernetes: Minikube manifests for local

CI/CD: GitHub Actions (lint, test, build, push image)

Monitoring: Prometheus + Grafana dashboards

Terraform: VPC + EKS provisioning templates